msgid ""
msgstr ""
"# Contributing to this project\n"
"\n"
"First of all, thank you for taking the time to contribute to this project. Your support is what makes the open-source community such an amazing place to learn, inspire, and create. We value your participation and look forward to working with you.\n"
"\n"
"The following is a set of guidelines for contributing to this project. These are just guidelines, not rules. Use your best judgment and feel free to propose changes to this document through a pull request.\n"
"\n"
"## Code of Conduct\n"
"\n"
"This project and everyone participating in it is governed by the [Code of Conduct](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md). By participating, you are expected to uphold this code. Please report unacceptable behavior to [project email].\n"
"\n"
"## How Can I Contribute?\n"
"\n"
"### Reporting Bugs\n"
"\n"
"This section guides you through submitting a bug report for this project. Following these guidelines helps maintainers and the community understand your report, reproduce the behavior, and find related reports.\n"
"\n"
"**Before Submitting A Bug Report**\n"
"\n"
"* Check the [FAQ](FAQ.md) for answers to common questions and problems.\n"
"* Check the [open issues](https://github.com/[project]/issues) for a list of known bugs.\n"
"* Perform a cursory search to see if the problem has already been reported. If it has and the issue is still open, add a comment to the existing issue instead of opening a new one.\n"
"\n"
"**How Do I Submit A (Good) Bug Report?**\n"
"\n"
"Bugs are tracked as [GitHub issues](https://guides.github.com/features/issues/). Create an issue and provide the following information by filling in [the template](ISSUE_TEMPLATE.md).\n"
"\n"
"Explain the problem and include additional details to help maintainers reproduce the problem:\n"
"\n"
"* **Use a clear and descriptive title** for the issue to identify the problem.\n"
"* **Describe the exact steps which reproduce the problem** in as many details as possible. For example, start by explaining how you started the project, e.g., which command exactly you used in the terminal, or how you created the project otherwise. When listing steps, **don't just say what you did, but explain how you did it**.\n"
"* **Provide specific examples to demonstrate the steps**. Include links to files or GitHub projects, or copy/pasteable snippets, which you use in those examples. If you're providing snippets in the issue, use [Markdown code blocks](https://help.github.com/articles/markdown-basics/#multiple-lines).\n"
"* **Describe the behavior you observed after following the steps** and point out what exactly is the problem with that behavior.\n"
"* **Explain which behavior you expected to happen instead and why.**\n"
"* **Include screenshots and animated GIFs** which show you following the described steps and clearly demonstrate the problem. You can use [this tool](https://www.cockos.com/licecap/) to record GIFs on macOS and Windows, and [this tool](https://github.com/colinkeenan/silentcast) or [this tool](https://github.com/GNOME/byzanz) on Linux.\n"
"* **If the problem is related to performance or memory**, include a CPU profile capture with your report.\n"
"* **If the problem wasn't triggered by a specific action**, describe what you were doing before the problem happened and share more information using the guidelines below.\n"
"\n"
"Provide more context by answering these questions:\n"
"\n"
"* **Did the problem start happening recently** (e.g., after updating to a new version of the project) or was this always a problem?\n"
"* If the problem started happening recently, **can you reproduce the problem in an older version of the project?** What's the most recent version in which the problem doesn't happen?\n"
"* **Can you reliably reproduce the issue?** If not, provide details about how often the problem happens and under which conditions it normally happens.\n"
"\n"
"Include details about your configuration and environment:\n"
"\n"
"* **Which version of the project are you using?**\n"
"* **What's the name and version of the OS you're using**?\n"
"* **Are you running the project in a virtual machine?** If so, which VM software are you using and which operating systems and versions are used for the host and the guest?\n"
"* **Which keyboard layout are you using?** Are you using a US layout or some other layout?\n"
"\n"
"### Submitting Code Changes\n"
"\n"
"This section guides you through submitting code changes for this project, including new features, improvements, and fixes. Following these guidelines helps maintainers and the community understand your submission, review it, and merge it.\n"
"\n"
"**Before Submitting A Code Change**\n"
"\n"
"* Check the [open issues](https://github.com/[project]/issues) for a list of known issues and feature requests.\n"
"* Check if your change has already been proposed or implemented by searching for related issues and pull requests.\n"
"* Perform a cursory search to see if the change has already been discussed. If it has and the discussion is still open, add a comment to the existing issue instead of opening a new one.\n"
"\n"
"**How Do I Submit A (Good) Code Change?**\n"
"\n"
"Changes are tracked as [GitHub pull requests](https://help.github.com/articles/about-pull-requests/). Create a pull request and provide the following information by filling in [the template](PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md).\n"
"\n"
"* **Use a clear and descriptive title** for the pull request to identify the change.\n"
"* **Explain the motivation for making this change**. For example, explain the problem the change solves, or the new feature it adds.\n"
"* **Explain the impact of the change**. For example, does it change existing behavior, or does it add new functionality?\n"
"* **Include screenshots and animated GIFs** which show the change in action, if applicable.\n"
"* **Describe the exact steps which are needed to test the change** in as many details as possible.\n"
"* **Make sure your code follows the style guidelines** of the project. See the [Style Guide](#style-guide) section for more details.\n"
"* **Make sure your code is well-tested**. See the [Testing](#testing) section for more details.\n"
"* **Make sure your code is well-documented**. See the [Documentation](#documentation) section for more details.\n"
"\n"
"## Style Guide\n"
"\n"
"### Git Commit Messages\n"
"\n"
"* Use the present tense (\"Add feature\" not \"Added feature\").\n"
"* Use the imperative mood (\"Move cursor to...\" not \"Moves cursor to...\").\n"
"* Limit the first line to 72 characters or less.\n"
"* Reference issues and pull requests liberally after the first line.\n"
"* Consider starting the commit message with an applicable emoji:\n"
"    * :art: `:art:` when improving the format/structure of the code\n"
"    * :racehorse: `:racehorse:` when improving performance\n"
"    * :memo: `:memo:` when writing docs\n"
"    * :bug: `:bug:` when fixing a bug\n"
"    * :fire: `:fire:` when removing code or files\n"
"    * :white_check_mark: `:white_check_mark:` when adding tests\n"
"    * :sparkles: `:sparkles:` when introducing a new feature\n"
"    * :recycle: `:recycle:` when refactoring code\n"
"    * :shirt: `:shirt:` when fixing lint warnings/errors\n"
"\n"
"### JavaScript Style Guide\n"
"\n"
"All JavaScript code is linted with [ESLint](https://eslint.org/). The configuration file is [.eslintrc](.eslintrc). Please make sure your code passes the linter before submitting a pull request.\n"
"\n"
"* Use semicolons.\n"
"* Use single quotes for strings, except to avoid escaping.\n"
"* Use 2 spaces for indentation.\n"
"* Use `const` for constants and `let` for variables that are reassigned. Avoid using `var`.\n"
"* Use template literals for string interpolation.\n"
"* Use arrow functions for anonymous functions.\n"
"* Use `===` and `!==` instead of `==` and `!=`.\n"
"* Use descriptive variable names. Avoid abbreviations and single-letter names, except for loop counters.\n"
"* Add a space after keywords (`if`, `for`, `while`, etc.) and after commas.\n"
"* Add a space before and after operators (`+`, `-`, `=`, etc.).\n"
"* Add a space before the opening brace of a block.\n"
"* Add a newline at the end of the file.\n"
"\n"
"### HTML/CSS Style Guide\n"
"\n"
"* Use 2 spaces for indentation.\n"
"* Use double quotes for HTML attributes.\n"
"* Use lowercase for HTML tags and attributes.\n"
"* Use meaningful class names that describe the content, not the presentation.\n"
"* Use CSS custom properties for colors and other repeated values.\n"
"* Add a newline at the end of the file.\n"
"\n"
"### Documentation Style Guide\n"
"\n"
"* Use [Markdown](https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/) for documentation.\n"
"* Use sentence case for headings and titles.\n"
"* Use descriptive link text, not \"click here\" or \"read more\".\n"
"* Use fenced code blocks with language identifiers for code examples.\n"
"* Use relative links for internal links.\n"
"\n"
"## Testing\n"
"\n"
"* Write unit tests for all new code and modify existing tests when changing behavior.\n"
"* Run the full test suite before submitting a pull request.\n"
"* Make sure all tests pass.\n"
"* If you are adding a new feature, add tests that cover the new functionality.\n"
"* If you are fixing a bug, add a test that reproduces the bug and verifies the fix.\n"
"\n"
"## Documentation\n"
"\n"
"* Update the documentation when adding or changing features.\n"
"* Update the [README](README.md) if the change affects the public API or usage.\n"
"* Update the [CHANGELOG](CHANGELOG.md) if the change is user-facing.\n"
"* Use clear and concise language.\n"
"* Use examples to illustrate usage.\n"
"\n"
"## What Happens After I Submit a Pull Request?\n"
"\n"
"* A maintainer will review your pull request and provide feedback.\n"
"* If the pull request is not ready to be merged, the maintainer will request changes.\n"
"* Once the pull request is approved, a maintainer will merge it.\n"
"* If the pull request is not accepted, the maintainer will close it with an explanation.\n"
"\n"
"## Additional Notes\n"
"\n"
"### Issue and Pull Request Labels\n"
"\n"
"This section lists the labels we use to help us track and manage issues and pull requests.\n"
"\n"
"* `bug` - A bug report.\n"
"* `enhancement` - A feature request.\n"
"* `documentation` - A documentation improvement.\n"
"* `good first issue` - A good issue for first-time contributors.\n"
"* `help wanted` - An issue that needs help from the community.\n"
"* `invalid` - An issue that is not valid.\n"
"* `question` - A question about the project.\n"
"* `wontfix` - An issue that will not be fixed.\n"
"\n"
"## Attribution\n"
"\n"
"This contributing guide is adapted from the [Atom project](https://github.com/atom/atom/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md) contributing guide, which is itself adapted from the [Contributing to Open Source](https://opensource.guide/how-to-contribute/) guide.\n"

